{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`docs/releasing.md` tells a release operator that the `pypi` GitHub environment's\nrequired-reviewers rule is \"the second human approval on every publish\" (line 76-77) and that\nstep 2 finishes by approving \"the `pypi` deployment when prompted\" (lines 82-83, 325). Both\noverstate the control. Verified read-only against the live GitHub API on 2026-08-19:\n\n- `GET /repos/navapbc/rebar/environments/pypi` returns a `required_reviewers` rule whose\n  `reviewers` list holds exactly **one** entry — the user `JoeOakhartNava`, which is this\n  project's configured release identity — with `prevent_self_review: false`, and the\n  environment itself has `can_admins_bypass: true`.\n- `GET /repos/navapbc/rebar/actions/runs/31916566035/approvals` (the 0.12.0 release) shows\n  `state: \"approved\"` cast by `JoeOakhartNava` — granted non-interactively through the\n  pending-deployments REST API, not by a person answering a UI prompt.\n\nSo the gate is neither a *second* approval (the sole required reviewer is the same identity\nthat dispatches the run, and self-review is permitted) nor an *interactive human* one. A\nrunbook that says otherwise teaches an operator to wait for a prompt that never arrives, or to\nbelieve a second party reviewed a release that in fact nobody but the dispatcher touched.\n\n## Approach\n\nCorrect the three passages in `docs/releasing.md` so they describe the mechanism that exists,\nwithout replacing one overstated claim with another. The corrected text must state:\n\n- **What the gate does guarantee** — the `publish` job, and therefore the environment-scoped\n  OIDC token that Trusted Publishing mints, is held until an identity on the `pypi`\n  environment's required-reviewers list explicitly approves that specific run. Combined with\n  the `main`-only deployment-branch policy and the `authorize` job's ref/ancestry guards, no\n  run from another ref and no run lacking that identity's approval can reach PyPI.\n- **What it does not guarantee** — it is not independent second-party review. The sole\n  required reviewer is the configured release identity, `prevent_self_review` is off (so the\n  dispatcher approves their own run), and repository admins can bypass the environment\n  (`can_admins_bypass: true`). The \"the published source was reviewed\" property comes from\n  releasing only from Gerrit-reviewed `main`, which the doc already states correctly — the\n  environment rule adds identity control over *who can release the publish job*, not a\n  content review.\n- **How to approve non-interactively**, since that is the path actually used: a complete\n  `gh api --method POST .../pending_deployments` invocation carrying its required body\n  (`environment_ids`, `state`, `comment`), plus the companion `GET .../pending_deployments`\n  that discovers the environment id. The bare path with no body returns 422. The body is\n  supplied with `--input -` and an explicit JSON document, **not** with `-f`/`-F` field flags:\n  `environment_ids` must serialize as a JSON array of integers, and `gh api -f\n  environment_ids=<n>` sends a string while `-F environment_ids='[<n>]'` sends the literal\n  string `[<n>]` — both are rejected by the API. An explicit JSON body removes that\n  flag-typing hazard from the runbook entirely.\n\nKeep the env-preflight description load-bearing and unchanged in substance: `release_guards.py`\nstill fails the release closed when the required-reviewers rule or the `main`-only branch\npolicy is missing. What changes is the description of who approves and how, never the guard.\n\n**Sweep decision (explicit).** `CONTRIBUTING.md` and `AGENTS.md` were grepped and contain\n**no** occurrence of this framing, so neither needs an edit; `CONTRIBUTING.md` stays in the\nfile-impact list only to record that the sweep covered it. `.github/workflows/release.yml`\nline 294 carries the identical claim in a comment (\"the second human gate on every publish\")\nand **is** in scope: leaving it would let the workflow source contradict the corrected runbook\none grep away. It is a comment-only edit; no test asserts that line.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `docs/releasing.md` — rewrite the \"Release-source authorization\" closing paragraph and\n  step 2's approval sentence; add the non-interactive approval recipe.\n- `.github/workflows/release.yml` — correct the line-294 `publish`-job comment to match.\n- `CONTRIBUTING.md` — swept, no occurrence found; no edit expected.\n\n## Testing\n\nDocumentation-only correction; it adds no testable behavior, so no new test is written. The\nexisting `tests/unit/test_release_workflow_pins.py` drift assertions over `docs/releasing.md`\nand `release.yml` are the regression oracle and must keep passing.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `docs/releasing.md` step 2 documents approving the pypi pending deployment as the\n      configured release identity and includes a complete, runnable REST form whose request\n      body types `environment_ids` as a JSON array of integers. Checked:\n      `grep -n \"pending_deployments\" docs/releasing.md` returns a hit, and\n      `grep -nE '\"environment_ids\": *\\[' docs/releasing.md` and\n      `grep -n '\"state\": \"approved\"' docs/releasing.md` each return a hit, while\n      `grep -nE '\\-f +environment_ids|\\-F +environment_ids' docs/releasing.md` returns\n      nothing (no string-typed field flag for the array).\n- [ ] The runbook also shows the companion read that discovers the environment id, so an\n      operator can fill the body without guessing. Checked:\n      `grep -n \"pending_deployments\" docs/releasing.md` returns at least one line that is a\n      `GET`/read invocation (`gh api repos/navapbc/rebar/actions/runs/.../pending_deployments`)\n      distinct from the `--method POST` line.\n- [ ] The \"Release-source authorization\" section no longer calls the environment rule a human\n      or second approval, and instead names both the guarantee (only a listed reviewer can\n      release the publish job for a run) and its limits (single configured reviewer,\n      self-review permitted, admin bypass). Checked:\n      `grep -nEi \"second human|human approval\" docs/releasing.md` returns nothing, and\n      `grep -nEi \"prevent.self.review|self-review|admin\" docs/releasing.md` returns a hit.\n- [ ] The env-preflight remains documented as load-bearing and fail-closed. Checked:\n      `grep -n \"fails closed\\|fail the release closed\\|fails the release closed\" docs/releasing.md`\n      still returns a hit, and `grep -n \"required_reviewers\" docs/releasing.md` still does.\n- [ ] No file in the repository retains the interactive-human-approval framing. Checked:\n      `grep -rnEi \"second human|human approval|approve the .?\\`?pypi\\`?. deployment when prompted\" docs/ CONTRIBUTING.md AGENTS.md .github/workflows/release.yml`\n      returns no matches. (The earlier literal `approve the pypi deployment` never matched:\n      the real text is backticked, `approve the \\`pypi\\` deployment`, so the pattern is\n      backtick-agnostic.)\n- [ ] Gates pass on the whole tree: `make lint`, `make typecheck`,\n      `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` reporting `new=0 increased=0\n      stale=0`, and `pytest tests/unit/test_release_workflow_pins.py`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787201441389748001,"uuid":"dc219af4-626a-4c25-aa79-4b7721e91adb"}